<Prose title="How We Score Home Theater Gear: The BHTR Score Method" description="How the BHTR Score works: spec-sheet analysis, room maths, independent measurements and owner-reported reliability, weighted by category. What we claim and what we don't." h1="How We Score: The BHTR Score" path="/how-we-test/" intro="Every product we cover gets a BHTR Score out of 100. Here is exactly what goes into it.">
  <h2>What the Score Is Built On</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>The spec sheet, read properly.</strong> We read past the headline numbers to the conditions behind them: receiver power at two channels driven vs all channels, ISO/ANSI lumens vs "LED lumens", subwoofer response at ±3 dB vs unspecified ranges.</li>
    <li><strong>Room maths.</strong> We convert specs into what they mean in a real room: throw distance for a 100–135 inch screen, amplifier power needed for reference-level peaks at your seat, sub output vs room volume. Our <a href="/tools/projector-throw-calculator/">throw</a> and <a href="/tools/viewing-distance-calculator/">viewing distance</a> calculators use the same formulas.</li>
    <li><strong>Independent measurements.</strong> Where reputable third-party measurements are published (for example frequency response or input lag), we use them to check manufacturer claims.</li>
    <li><strong>Owner-reported reliability.</strong> We read owner reviews and forum reports for recurring failures, firmware problems and support issues over time.</li>
    <li><strong>Value.</strong> Every product is scored against its price band, so a great budget sub can outrank an expensive mediocre one.</li>
  </ol>
  <h2>Category Weightings</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>What we weight most</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Projectors</td><td>Contrast and black level, brightness (ISO/ANSI), HDR tone mapping, lens flexibility (zoom/shift), input lag, value</td></tr>
      <tr><td>AV receivers</td><td>Real channel count and Atmos layouts, room correction, HDMI 2.1 inputs, pre-outs, power for the money</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Speakers</td><td>Frequency response and dispersion, sensitivity/impedance (how easy to drive), timbre matching across the range, build</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Subwoofers</td><td>Low-frequency extension and output for room size, distortion at volume, EQ/app control, size</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Soundbars and systems</td><td>True channel count vs virtualization, Atmos and DTS:X support, expandability, eARC, room correction</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <h2>What We Don't Claim</h2>
  <p>We do not run an acoustics lab, and we never say "we tested" unless we have. Our scores are a structured research verdict, not bench measurements. When we add hands-on impressions in future, we will say so clearly on the page.</p>
  <h2>Score Bands</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>90–100:</strong> class-leading; rare.</li>
    <li><strong>80–89:</strong> excellent; an easy recommendation for the right buyer.</li>
    <li><strong>70–79:</strong> good with clear trade-offs.</li>
    <li><strong>60–69:</strong> only for specific needs or at a discount.</li>
    <li><strong>Below 60:</strong> we'd buy something else.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Updates</h2>
  <p>We review our best lists regularly, and whenever a major model launches or is discontinued. The "Updated" date on each page shows the last substantive revision.</p>
</Prose>
